task: remove-codecs-maxdepth
agent: opus-depth
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/remove-codecs-maxdepth
worktree: ../okay-wt-remove-maxdepth
spec: specs/iterative-recursive-decode.md ("the arc closed" — the
  deliberate follow-up it named but left undone)

why: the operator's own call, after the arc closed: with all six
  doors stack-safe past NativeThreshold, Codecs.maxDepth has no
  stack-safety motivation left, so its wire-contract refusal is
  removed outright (raising it was the other option; REMOVE was
  chosen). Flagged once before starting: removing the cap does not
  make depth free — a Cont.defer trampoline still allocates a node
  per level and the decoded value tree still lives on the heap, so an
  adversarially deep message now costs HEAP instead of native stack.
  Operator's call, made with that stated.

what: delete `Codecs.maxDepth`, `Json.maxDepth`, `Json.isCut`,
  `Json.tooDeep`, `Json.cutMessage`, and every refusal built on them
  (`Cbor.In.enter`/`tooDeep`, `JsonStrict.Reader.enter`/`tooDeep`,
  `JsonValue`'s `open >= Json.maxDepth` guard, the cut-propagation
  rules `cut-refuses-the-document` added to `Json.decode`/`Staged`/
  `RuntimeStaged`, `skipValue`'s depth-capped bracket counter).
  `Codecs.NativeThreshold` (the native/trampoline switch — unrelated
  to the refusal, still needed) stays untouched. Every test built on
  "past maxDepth refuses" either deletes (the refusal it proved no
  longer exists) or becomes a positive "decodes correctly at a real,
  large depth" test — decided file by file, not assumed here.

predict: this touches every file the whole iterative-recursive-decode
  arc touched, plus the tests from BEFORE that arc that first proved
  the refusal (input-depth-both-wires, cut-refuses-the-document,
  lower-maxdepth-real-margin). Large, mechanical, no new design — the
  design question was already answered by the arc; this lane removes
  what it made unnecessary.
